WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.600
Film Courage: What are the top three reasons
a filmmaker will be rejected by a film festival?

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.500
Daniel Sol, Hollyshorts Film Festival Co-Founder:
Well I think one, first thing something that

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.570
(I’ve been thinking about this a little
more) at times it’s not the fault of any

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.209
filmmaker at all.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.690
Some festivals and at times we even see it
with our’s you have a story that you’re

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.820
telling that is redundant in the sense that
it’s a category that is very heavy in a

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.449
sort of area or style of storytelling.

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:29.489
It can be sometimes dramas.

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:37.050
Or it may be a sort of heavy family drama
that might be replicated by 10 other films

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.680
or maybe more that are telling a very similar
story.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.839
Now if that happens as programmers we are
looking to program a diverse range of films

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:52.929
and are we going to show four, five or six
of the same types of stories and films?

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:55.790
And if we are only selecting 400 out of thousands
of films.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.960
That’s going to be very hard to say yes
to that.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.429
And it’s not fault of anyone’s.

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:05.750
It’s just a situation of percentages, you
are telling a story that happens to be similar

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.230
without getting into to many details of the
specific stories (of an exact story).

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.230
You know what I’m saying?

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:18.120
It falls under something that we are seeing
in this whole submission pool, identical stories

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.370
essentially.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:30.540
Sometimes even very identical to the point
where the filmmaker takes a story that is

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.410
in the public domain and remakes a story whether
it is Stephen King or something like that

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.410
right?

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.570
And they tell that story, you might see four
submissions of the same exact movie which

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:40.570
is fascinating from our side.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.770
Hey who did it better or who did it worse?

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.750
They are all different movies but they are
the same story, they are literally the same

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:48.750
story.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.320
So at that point are we going to show all
four of them even if they are all great?

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.120
It’s hard to say yes to that.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.500
We’re not going to show the same movie over
and over.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.930
We’d had it happen where actually where
two films that are actually the same films

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.070
actually made it in.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.000
Same stories but actually different types
of films to be made.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.890
Different types of films but same story.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.250
So that can happen, it’s rare but it has
happened.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.840
Most of the time they are not going to get
in.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.090
It’s hard to say if they are all great maybe
none of them get in.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.110
What if one gets in?

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:20.780
Are we going to accept two more generally
speaking of the same story?

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.540
That’s not something you can control as
a filmmaker?

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.090
How do you know you made a film and that someone
else made the same film and they submitted

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.090
it?

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.090
That’s not something you would know.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.380
That’s not something you can control?

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.870
So there’s an element of uncontrollability
to that.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:38.910
Then there are also the percentages as I mentioned
we went from the early years (2,300, 70, 500,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.760
1000) to know 5,000 plus submissions.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.940
Same amount of slots, we are not increasing
the festival to 800 slots.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.620
We’re not going to 14, 15 days.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.620
We are 10 days.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.600
We are around 400 films give or take give
on run time.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.530
And five programs a day and so within that
we have different categories.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:16.830
We have to fill in the blanks as to what can
fit based on genre, need and style.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.980
So that’s already inherently a competitive
situation.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:26.640
We’re talking 5 to maybe 10 percent of all
submissions will generally get accepted.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.849
So now inherently we are saying potentially
90% may not even make it.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:34.459
We’re not even talking about scores, we’re
not talking about anything else.

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:40.319
I’m talking about just the numbers there
so that’s the honest portrayal of the competition

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:41.780
nature.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.030
And now outside of that we get into what is
wrong?

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:52.550
Now we are looking at any of the potential
flaws whether it is technical stuff, technical

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:59.390
issues whether it’s the obvious things of
poor jump cuts or a boom mic in shots (obvious

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:00.390
things).

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.050
Any of those things, that’s it.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:06.050
That’s kind of an automatic rejection of
sorts.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:10.430
Basically you are eliminating yourself in
that sense just because of those errors or

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.430
edits.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.680
So there is that.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.789
Then you get into how is the acting?

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:19.840
How is the storytelling?

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:27.879
Then if some of them are really poor you are
going to have a lower score you are going

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:28.879
to get reject.

00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:29.879
You are not going to make it.

00:04:29.879 --> 00:04:32.400
And again, that’s the standard baseline
of our festival at this time.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.400
I don’t know about other festivals.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.659
Maybe there are less submissions or more slots
and other things that they have going on.

00:04:37.659 --> 00:04:45.749
But when you look at that aspect of it some
of those elements can make it automatic for

00:04:45.749 --> 00:04:47.789
us so you are sort of rejecting yourself in
some sense.

00:04:47.789 --> 00:04:52.490
If some of those areas are flawed and then
there are some of the technical aspects those

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:53.720
things are sort of automatic.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:58.189
Then you get into after all that what are
we looking to program?

00:04:58.189 --> 00:04:59.190
How is the storytelling?

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.540
Is it unique?

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.130
That’s a big thing.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:07.610
We are looking for at times watching so many
films our review team and myself watching

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:16.289
so many films it’s always exciting and something
desirable that a film and a story whether

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:21.900
it’s outside-of-the-box or different, stands
out in a big way.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:28.540
That can be a lot of things whether it’s
very graphic whatever or a unique style of

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:32.940
storytelling or visually very different or
whatever that may mean.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.319
But that’s a big deal.

00:05:34.319 --> 00:05:39.249
Because you look at all these films and if
something rises to the top you say “I’ve

00:05:39.249 --> 00:05:40.249
never seen this before?

00:05:40.249 --> 00:05:41.249
This is very unique.”

00:05:41.249 --> 00:05:42.319
And we’ve never seen this before.

00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:45.629
We’ve seen all these films that’s something
that is obviously going to weigh on our minds.

00:05:45.629 --> 00:05:47.180
We’re not going to forget that whether it’s
very graphic or whatever.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:51.639
So I guess a long-winded way of saying this
is try not to be forgettable, make it unforgettable.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:54.659
Make it something that just wows everybody
watching.

00:05:54.659 --> 00:05:57.490
Where you walk away saying “Wow, I can’t
believe that film.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:58.729
That was amazing.”

00:05:58.729 --> 00:06:05.380
Because there are many stories that I guess
are playing it safe where the filmmaker or

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:10.639
storyteller is not really trying to push boundaries
or not trying to do something different, maybe

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:12.080
it’s just another filmmaking style.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.460
Maybe there is just a lot of two-shots.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.990
Now I know there are budget restraints of
course but within that you have to be creative

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.129
and show your style.

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:25.139
If it’s not how is that going to stand out
against a crowd of all these films.

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:30.759
If you don’t, your chances of being accepted
at that point become very, very low.

00:06:30.759 --> 00:06:33.069
The numbers game is there, it’s very difficult.

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:42.740
So without talking technical stuff, the big
when films are too (for lack of a better word)

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:46.960
“Vanilla” you’re shooting yourself in
the foot (for us at least).

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.800
Definitely we’ve seen so many films, if
nothing stands out about it how is it going

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:51.990
to be unforgettable for us?

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:56.139
If it’s not unforgettable, then we are just
probably just going to forget it in a sense

00:06:56.139 --> 00:06:57.139
right?

00:06:57.139 --> 00:06:58.139
So that’s important.

00:06:58.139 --> 00:07:04.050
Film Courage: Do you think people are taking
less risks or more risks in terms of the storytelling

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.050
aspect?

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.560
Sort of being these agent provocateurs, putting
stuff out there that is like “Whoah!”

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.740
Or some people are going to be really offended
by it.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.210
Or do you think it is less so?

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.210
Have we become so sensitive?

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:17.809
Are we too P.C.?

00:07:17.809 --> 00:07:18.809
Daniel: Maybe?

00:07:18.809 --> 00:07:21.020
Well that’s always tough to say.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.550
Because we see a lot of stuff that’s not
stuff, so I’d say no then.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:31.190
But then we are sort of living in this new
age of being checked online (PC culture) of

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:34.389
“Don’t say that, don’t say this, don’t
do that.”

00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:38.680
Of course we know there is this online battle
going on where it is cyberbullying or mob

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.520
mentality of people jumping on people for
saying the wrong thing.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:46.449
I still don’t think that plays much into
the films.

00:07:46.449 --> 00:07:50.919
I still think people are trying to push boundaries
and tell really great stories and share what

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:55.379
they believe in and share their vision and
style because again this also goes back to

00:07:55.379 --> 00:08:00.520
what is shorts (always were and still are)
in some degree a calling card.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.189
You are trying to say “Hey, look at me world.

00:08:03.189 --> 00:08:09.220
This is what I can do, I’m showing my ability,
this is the restraints I’m up against.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:14.199
This is how much money I have, I’ve only
got 10 grand, this is what I can make with

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:15.740
it and I can kick-ass film.”

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:25.990
Well if that’s what you’re trying to do
that, if you are making a seven minute short

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:31.770
that is a drama that we’ve seen before retold,
a certain story that has been done many times

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:39.650
by many filmmakers and there is nothing original
to it, you’re not going to see many acceptances

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:41.839
to it.

00:08:41.839 --> 00:08:45.410
So you really want to see people take a chance
whether it is something that is provocative

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:51.009
or adventurous or just out there or it is
political or something that is trying to make

00:08:51.009 --> 00:08:54.089
a stance or a stand or a strong point.

00:08:54.089 --> 00:08:59.810
You’ve still got to try to show that voice,
you’ve got to do it.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.830
All that said though some people do want to
make what they want to make.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:06.279
You have to make your film, you can’t try
to make something or be something you are

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:09.760
not so there always is that.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.720
But there is always how you tell the simplest
of stories, how well you do it visually, how

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.899
you showcase your abilities is what it’s
about because we are trying to showcase what

00:09:17.899 --> 00:09:20.120
we feel are the best films in the world.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.670
That’s the goal, that’s the idea.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:25.860
So if it doesn’t fall into that or it doesn’t
feel like it belongs there or it’s that

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:31.040
exciting why are we going to put all of our
chips on it or really push it all forward

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.750
and showcase and say this is what has to be
accepted.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:37.410
These other ones got rejected, you take the
place of that film.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.620
You have to convince us.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:40.970
Make us say “Why?”

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.360
So that’s part of it.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.880
Film Courage: There was one film that I can
still see it to this day.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.649
It was about a woman who had been assaulted
and she ended up having a child, loved that

00:09:51.649 --> 00:09:56.990
child and the child finds out that it’s
a product of assault but I mean that film

00:09:56.990 --> 00:10:00.779
was unavailable and I realize that some people
[filmmakers] would be very scared to do that

00:10:00.779 --> 00:10:02.240
[topic] especially today.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.730
But I thought it was amazing.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:10.500
I know you guys have shown some things that
really push boundaries but there are reasons

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.500
for that and they are really amazing.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:20.569
Daniel: For us it has been a joy and we are
in a position where we have the opportunity

00:10:20.569 --> 00:10:25.370
to dictate what we want to do with the festival,
with the style, with everything, parties,

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:30.959
events, whatever part of planning the event,
whatever gets screened, it’s exciting to

00:10:30.959 --> 00:10:33.210
obviously have a say in something in this
world right?

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:38.570
Where we say this is the best way to do this
and we believe in this and we can do that.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:43.790
With that comes opportunity and also responsibility
to try to do the best we can with it.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:44.790
And if we are giving a stamp of approval.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:48.340
We are sort of trying to be as many festivals
believed to be the gatekeepers, saying this

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:50.250
is what we need to show the audience.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.721
This is what you need to pay attention to.

00:10:51.721 --> 00:10:54.630
Hollywood look at these filmmakers and these
films because this is what we believe are

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:57.930
the best of the best, what we believe you
need to look at, these are the films that

00:10:57.930 --> 00:10:58.930
you need to pay attention to.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:10:59.930
That’s what we’re doing.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.269
We are shining a light on these films.

00:11:01.269 --> 00:11:04.990
Obviously we are saying these ones, come take
a look at these, the other ones didn’t quite

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:08.060
make it (this is the reason or whatever) but
these are the ones we believe in strongly

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.399
and you have to come see these movies.

00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:16.040
This is a responsibility that we also have
to take seriously, not just throw up anything

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:20.069
on screen because we owe someone something
or something.

00:11:20.069 --> 00:11:23.399
That’s not what it’s about.

00:11:23.399 --> 00:11:29.700
I think the more films and filmmakers are
pushing those boundaries.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:34.910
A lot of films are doing like Sundance and
they program a lot of stuff that you see over

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:39.899
the years has also pushed boundaries, have
been very unique and aggressive in their storytelling,

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:44.290
their styles and not pulling back or holding
back for fear of what people react to or what

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:45.290
they will say.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:49.069
Especially if there is something like a voice
you want to really share, what you have to

00:11:49.069 --> 00:11:53.949
say, what you believe in, it doesn’t make
sense to be restrained or pulled back just

00:11:53.949 --> 00:11:57.220
based on fear of reaction or what people might
think of you or something.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:02.389
That’s not going to work and if you do that
your chances of being that many festivals

00:12:02.389 --> 00:12:04.420
or any are now very low at that point.

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:10.000
Yes, you’ve got to tell the story you’ve
got to tell but that also means if it is more

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:14.350
of a film that doesn’t require that kind
of…it’s not a provocative story or things

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:15.350
like that.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.180
It can be more of a simple kids film or something,
that’s fine.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:22.019
But if it’s the opposite or it’s supposed
to be this very provocative story, don’t

00:12:22.019 --> 00:12:24.740
also pull the curtain back or pull back a
bit.

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:31.500
I’m trying to think of a quote that I saw
recently, it was dealing of that same subject

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:38.080
of when it came to a film…it was retelling
a war story and they were saying there is

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:42.339
responsibility to not pull back on how aggressive
and awful that is.

00:12:42.339 --> 00:12:46.660
If you are not showing what that really is
you are doing that disservice, the people

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:47.750
think “Oh this is okay.

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.829
This is not that bad.”

00:12:49.829 --> 00:12:51.339
You can’t walk away feeling that way.

00:12:51.339 --> 00:12:54.149
That’s not what we should be portraying
as a situation of showing what it means to

00:12:54.149 --> 00:12:55.500
be in war.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.250
War is violent, awful, terrible and sad.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:02.040
To make that feel like it’s not, that’s
a disservice.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:05.689
That’s something that I think people should
really keep in mind and that’s something

00:13:05.689 --> 00:13:09.130
I’ve always kind of thought of when I think
back to that quote.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:12.769
I forget, I can’t actually remember the
exact quote, so I’m paraphrasing someone

00:13:12.769 --> 00:13:13.769
of something I saw recently.

00:13:13.769 --> 00:13:17.189
And I remember it stuck with me in a documentary
I was watching.

00:13:17.189 --> 00:13:23.720
It’s very important to show how awful things
like that can be and if you’re telling that

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:29.170
story, not tell it in a PC way to make sure
it is what it’s supposed to be and it is

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:33.240
aggressive and things like that or whether
it is violent or sad or depressing and not

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:34.439
make it less depressing and not make it less
depressing.

00:13:34.439 --> 00:13:39.370
You mentioned the thing about assault, like
don’t make that be something that is not

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:44.110
awful or sad or make it feel like it’s just
okay.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:47.640
Then that’s just doing total disservice
to anyone who has ever been a victim of anything,

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:53.050
that’s very important to not have it be
the case so the long-winded answer is just

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.600
make sure to go all the way.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:01.569
Film Courage: And the filmmakers spoke afterwards
and they went into…I won’t give away too

00:14:01.569 --> 00:14:06.620
much…hopefully we can put a link to it at
the bottom of this video.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:12.970
It was amazingly done and I went up to the
filmmakers afterward and thanked them because

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.970
it was an incredible story.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:19.910
And although they were trying to show that
this mother loved this child, they had to

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.749
go to certain places that might make certain
people a little bit uncomfortable.

00:14:23.749 --> 00:14:26.999
Daniel: And challenging for those actors involved
in the project.

00:14:26.999 --> 00:14:33.470
And anything worth telling, it needs to be
that and it’s exciting when you see that.

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:36.910
For us, it’s exciting, like we have a chance
to actually show this and let the story be

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.149
told and give it sort of a life if it’s
a world premiere and it’s like we get to

00:14:40.149 --> 00:14:44.220
show this to the audience and this is the
work they’ve put in and the chances they’ve

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:46.279
taken and now we get to showcase that.

00:14:46.279 --> 00:14:51.149
That’s pretty cool and obviously something
that we’re able to do here.

00:14:51.149 --> 00:14:55.499
This is part of the blessing of being able
to do this work is showcase films like that

00:14:55.499 --> 00:15:00.339
giving them their first chance or whatever.

00:15:00.339 --> 00:15:03.279
Of if they’ve shown their movie 20 times
already and we’re like the 21st screening.

00:15:03.279 --> 00:15:07.060
But we’re getting a chance to show it to
our audience and another audience can see

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.879
it and that’s awesome so obviously we don’t
take it for granted.

